  <div class="wrap article-layout"><article class="article-body">
    <p class="lead">Mental health affects people of every age and background, yet it is still stigmatized, minimized, or overlooked. Unmasked Culture was created to challenge that pattern through vulnerability, authenticity, and a more complete understanding of well-being.</p>

    <h2>A mission rooted in lived experience</h2>
    <p>Unmasked Culture began in Minneapolis in 2019 with a mission to promote holistic mental health through multimedia and a community where people can be honest about what they are carrying.</p>
    <p>The mission is deeply personal to founder Mikel Stull. His own mental-health experiences—and the loss of his brother to suicide in 2020—strengthened his conviction that people need spaces where struggle can be named without shame.</p>

    <h2>From one vulnerable story to a growing community</h2>
    <p>The foundation began with a video in which Mikel shared his mental-health struggles openly. That first act of vulnerability grew into a YouTube series called <em>Vulnerability</em>, helping viewers recognize that they were not alone in their own experiences.</p>
    <blockquote class="pullquote">When one person removes the mask, someone else may finally feel safe enough to do the same.</blockquote>
    <p>The response revealed a clear need: people were looking for honest stories, practical resources, and a community willing to make room for difficult conversations.</p>

    <h2>Why holistic mental health matters</h2>
    <p>Traditional mental-health care can sometimes focus narrowly on symptoms. A holistic approach asks us to look at the whole person—body, mind, relationships, environment, purpose, and spirit—and to notice how those dimensions affect one another.</p>
    <p>This does not reject clinical care. Therapy, medication, crisis services, and other professional interventions can be essential. Holistic mental health expands the picture by also considering daily routines, community support, sleep, nutrition, movement, faith or meaning, financial stress, relationships, and the conditions in which someone lives.</p>

    <h2>Changing the conversation</h2>
    <p>Stigma thrives in secrecy. By shedding light on ordinary struggles and honoring vulnerability, Unmasked Culture works to make mental-health conversations more human and less isolating.</p>
    <p>Through podcasts, blogs, videos, programs, and community connections, the foundation carries a simple message: no one should have to navigate their mental-health journey alone. Every story shared with care can help replace judgment with understanding and silence with support.</p>

    <h2>An invitation to live unmasked</h2>
    <p>Breaking stigma is not a single campaign. It happens whenever someone listens without rushing to fix, asks for help without apologizing, speaks honestly about a hard season, or makes room for another person’s truth.</p>
    <p>Unmasked Culture invites each of us to participate—to care for our own whole-person well-being, support those around us, and build communities where authenticity is met with compassion.</p>
